Let $GW (p)$ be the Galton-Watson tree with the offspring distribution $p$. Let $m \in (1,\infty)$ be the finite mean of $p$. (We let $m>1$ so that $GW(p)$ itself is supercritical.) Consider the single-type SIR process on $GW(p)$. We say that it \textit{survives} if for all $t\geq 0$, there is at least one infected vertex at time $t$. The following result is classic.

\begin{table}[h]
 \label{rates table}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{c|c|c|c}
\hline
& original infection rate & increased infection rate & recovery rate\\
\hline 
disease $A$ & $\alpha_1$ & $\beta_1\in [\alpha_1,\infty]$ & $\mu_1$\\
\hline 
disease $B$ & $\alpha_2$ & $\beta_2\in [\alpha_2, \infty]$ & $\mu_2$\\
