\label{sec:intro}

\subsection{What is known}
\label{sec:intro_what_is_known}

Dualizing complexes play a central role in Grothendieck duality. For schemes and algebraic spaces, they occur in the study of singularities and derived categories. As algebraic stacks arise naturally in moduli theory and birational geometry, it is important to develop a comparable duality theory in this broader setting. However, unlike the case of schemes and algebraic spaces, general existence results for dualizing complexes on algebraic stacks remain limited.

Significant progress has been made with extending Grothendieck duality to algebraic stacks. In particular, \cite{Neeman:2023} established a functorial formalism for upper shriek functors $f^!$ using the notion of concentrated morphisms $f\colon \mathcal{Y}\to \mathcal{X}$ introduced by \cite{Hall/Rydh:2017}. Concentrated morphisms form a class of morphisms with favorable cohomological properties. For example, if $f\colon \mathcal{Y}\to \mathcal{X}$ is a concentrated morphism of algebraic stacks, then the derived pushforward $\mathbf{R}f_\ast \colon D_{\operatorname{qc}}(\mathcal{Y}) \to D_{\operatorname{qc}}(\mathcal{X})$ admits a right adjoint $f^{\times}$. In general, the functor $f^\times$ does not always coincide with $f^!$. 

For schemes, dualizing complexes are frequently constructed using upper shriek functors associated to structural morphisms rather than via gluing arguments. In the case of a proper morphism $f\colon Y \to X$ of Noetherian schemes, $f^!$ is naturally isomorphic to $f^\times$. This identification can make certain arguments easier in the scheme setting.

Motivated by this perspective, one may attempt to define a dualizing complex on an algebraic stack $\mathcal{X}$ as $f^{\times}\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{S}}$ for a suitable concentrated morphism $f\colon \mathcal{X}\to \mathcal{S}$. This viewpoint appears in \cite[Definition 1.22]{Nironi:2009} and behaves as
expected in certain cases. However, this construction does not work in general;
for instance, it fails for separated \'{e}tale morphisms between affine Noetherian schemes that are not Gorenstein.

\subsection{What we do}
\label{sec:intro_what_we_do}

In this work, dualizing complexes are defined on the lisse-\'etale site of the algebraic stack. Roughly, these are complexes which are dualizing smooth locally. While this notion is not new (see e.g.\ \cite[Remark 3.20(2)]{Enokizono/Hashizume:2026}), we formalize it a bit more carefully. See \Cref{sec:dualizing} for details.

