pre_theorem_intro_text:
Let $(\Omega,g)$ be a Jordan domain which is diffeomorphic to a two-dimensional closed disk and has a boundary $\partial \Omega =\Sigma$. We choose the orthornomal frame $\{e_1,e_2\}$ so that $e_1$ is tangent to $\Sigma$ and $e_2$ is normal to $\Sigma$. The boundary curve $\Sigma$ is parameterised as
\begin{equation}
[0,2\pi) \ni \varphi \mapsto i(\varphi) \in \Sigma. \end{equation}
Denote by $L=2\pi r_0$ and $k=g(\nabla^g_{e_1}e_2,e_1)$ the length and the geodesic curvature of $\Sigma$ with respect to the outward normal $e_2$ of $\Sigma$ respectively.

If $g$ is the standard Euclidean metric $g^E$, then
\begin{equation}
\int_\Sigma k \mathrm{d} s=2\pi.\end{equation}
This is the well-known rotation index theorem of a plane curve \cite{doC}.

Now suppose that the metric $g$ has nonnegative Gauss curvature $K_g$. Is there any good estimate for the total geodesic curvature $\int_\Sigma k \mathrm{d} s$? It immediately follows from the Gauss-Bonnet formula \cite{doC},
\begin{equation}
\int_\Sigma k \mathrm{d} s = 2\pi -\int_{\Omega} K_g \mathrm{d} g \leq 2\pi.\end{equation}

This result can also be viewed from the conformal perspective. Let $f$ be the solution to the Dirichlet problem of the following Poisson equation:
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
\Delta_g f  &= \ -K_g  \ \mbox{in} \  \Omega , \\
\ f  &= \ 0  \ \mbox{on} \  \Sigma ,
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $\Delta_g$ and $K_g$ are the Laplacian and the Gauss curvature of the metric $g$. Then the conformal metric $\bar g=e^{2f}g$ has zero Gauss curvature and hence must be Euclidean.  Note that $K_g$ is assumed to be nonnegative, by the Hopf lemma, the curvature $\bar k=\bar g(\bar\nabla_{\bar {e}_1}\bar e_2,\bar e_1)$ with respect to the metric $\bar g$ is no less than that of the the metric $g$. Therefore,
\begin{equation}
\int_\Sigma k \mathrm{d} s \leq \int_\Sigma \bar k \mathrm{d} s=2\pi.\end{equation}

It is natural to ask what we can say if the Gauss curvature has a negative lower bound. In this case the above technique encounters difficulties since the source term in the Poisson equation has no definite sign. However the Gauss-Bonnet formula still gives an upper bound,
\begin{equation}
\int_\Sigma k \mathrm{d} s = 2\pi +\int_{\Omega} \big(-K_g\big) \mathrm{d} g \leq 2\pi + |\Omega|_g\end{equation}
where $|\Omega|_g$ is the area of $\Omega$ with respect to the metric $g$, if assuming $K_g \ge -1$. This is a result concerning the effect of the curvature of the boundary curve curvature on the internal geometry of the bounded domain.  Note that the upper bound involves the 2-geometry of the entire $\Omega$.

Is it possible to give an upper bound of the total geodesic curvature which only involves the boundary geometry of $\Sigma$? The present work is of this nature. We will study the boundary behaviour of a compact 2-surface whose Gauss curvature is allowed to be negative. The main theorem in this paper is as follows.

