To prove the main theorem, one argues as follows. By \cite[Lemma 2.3]{MSZ2020}, the weak type $(1,1)$ estimate 
\begin{equation}\label{main:var}
		\left|\left\{x\in\R^d:\;V_q(T_{\Omega,\epsilon}f(x))>\alpha\right\}\right|\lesssim\frac{\|f\|_1}{\alpha}.
	\end{equation}
for the $q$-variation operator follows from the corresponding weak type estimate 
\begin{equation}\label{main:jump}
		\left|\left\{x\in\R^d:\lambda[N_\lambda(T_{\Omega,\epsilon} f(x))]^\frac{1}{2}>\alpha\right\}\right|\lesssim\frac{\|f\|_1}{\alpha}.
	\end{equation}
for the jump function. To prove 
\begin{equation}\label{main:jump}
		\left|\left\{x\in\R^d:\lambda[N_\lambda(T_{\Omega,\epsilon} f(x))]^\frac{1}{2}>\alpha\right\}\right|\lesssim\frac{\|f\|_1}{\alpha}.
	\end{equation}
it is enough to treat the short and long jump operators separately using the inequality
\begin{align}\label{shortlong}
	\lambda\left[N_\frac{\lambda}{2}\left(\sum_{s\geq5}\sum_{j\in\Z}(H_j^s\chi_{|\cdot|>\epsilon})*B^{j-s}(x):\epsilon\in (0,\infty)\right)\right]^\frac{1}{2}\lesssim&~\mathcal{S}_2\left(\sum_{s\geq5}\sum_{j\in\Z}(H_j^s\chi_{|\cdot|>\epsilon})*B^{j-s}(x):\epsilon\in (0,\infty)\right)\nonumber\\
	&+\lambda\left[N_\frac{\lambda}{2}\left(\sum_{s\geq5}\sum_{j\in\Z}(H_j^s\chi_{|\cdot|>2^k})*B^{j-s}(x):k\in\Z\right)\right]^\frac{1}{2},
\end{align}
In both cases, a key ingredient is the variational Rademacher--Menshov theorem (in the form of \cite[Lemma 7.2]{DOP2017}, stated as \Cref{VarRM}). A second ingredient is Seeger’s microlocal estimates \cite{Seeger1996}; the proof combines Seeger’s $L^1$ and $L^2$ estimates “into a single $L^2$ estimate” via “a careful interpolation argument” motivated by \cite{Honzik2020} (details given later).

The short/long jump proofs diverge in their scale structure: short jumps use “a single scale argument” reminiscent of \cite{CJRW2003}, while long jumps require “multiscale analysis” and an “algorithm that controls the interaction of scales,” following Krause--Lacey \cite{KL2018,KL2020} (cf. also \cite{IKM2025}). In adapting \cite{KL2020}, the proof is streamlined by “do[ing] away with the recursion argument” and by introducing “an effective decomposition of the bad functions based on the shifted dyadic grids,” which enables using the $L^2$ bounds (the auxiliary lemma above) as a black box. Specifically, we use the following lemma.

\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:Seegercombined}
		Let $\alpha>0$ and $\mathcal{Q}$ be a collection of cubes with disjoint interior such that for each $Q\in\mathcal{Q}$ there exists a function $b_Q\in L^1(Q)$ such that
		\[\int|b_Q(x)|dx\leq\alpha|Q|.\]
		Let $B_k=\sum_{Q\in\mathcal{Q}:|Q|=2^{dk}}b_Q$. Then, there exists $\delta>0$ such that for $s\geq5$, we have
		\[\left\|\sum_{j\in\Z}H_j*B^{j-s}\right\|_2^2\lesssim\mathscr{M}^22^{-s\delta}\alpha\sum_{Q\in\mathcal{Q}}\|b_Q\|_1.\]
	\end{lemma}

Finally, “by a standard limiting argument,” this completes the proof of the main theorem and recovers the weak $(1,1)$ boundedness of $T_\Omega^*$.

